utional context"}], "description": "Gist: Writers make stronger choices when they understand what the situation asks the text to do."}, {"id": "d45607f1-da3f-4103-b69f-187d5f103ef9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Kairos and rhetorical timing", "children": [{"id": "c320e374-6be7-4d51-96ab-f455eba47b54", "type": "concept", "title": "Timeliness"}, {"id": "2595e828-922b-4728-9716-cfccb000af1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Opportune moments"}, {"id": "a76ef5aa-0a04-4920-9b79-1ce145d190de", "type": "concept", "title": "Public events"}, {"id": "d4b7a1f6-9d1c-466f-9bde-63e372239fcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Response and intervention"}], "description": "Gist: Kairos helps students see why timing, urgency, and moment shape persuasive force."}]}, {"id": "c40d8a9d-5736-44b7-8e56-f78672268616", "type": "topic", "title": "Classical Rhetoric and Rhetorical Appeals", "description": "Use classical concepts such as ethos, pathos, logos, kairos, arrangement, and style to understand persuasive writing.", "children": [{"id": "478e9651-46d9-41ad-a170-d3b4accb3b6f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethos, pathos, and logos", "children": [{"id": "c8a90ab6-9beb-42b8-8601-6e8ff799583f", "type": "concept", "title": "Character and authority"}, {"id": "e47d0b1e-1df4-4073-a96c-95e8e748c956", "type": "concept", "title": "Emotional resonance"}, {"id": "539b390d-5bd5-4457-a8e4-894cd7086b31", "type": "concept", "title": "Reasoning and evidence"}, {"id": "518f9913-e199-4234-a863-72a8dd17585c", "type": "concept", "title": "Balance among appeals"}], "description": "Gist: The appeals provide a durable vocabulary for credibility, emotion, reasoning, and audience trust."}, {"id": "2bef5c29-b978-45d9-ba51-5f87eb97bfbc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Arrangement and invention", "children": [{"id": "28e3748b-175a-4376-83dd-4ae2f7d03918", "type": "concept", "title": "Invention heuristics"}, {"id": "b58b2d73-11ff-4a7b-9709-326fc1cd808f", "type": "concept", "title": "Exordium and narration"}, {"id": "6c849adf-c01a-46d0-ba90-8b836c3c02fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Confirmation and refutation"}, {"id": "30fb3c0c-3d21-4052-8097-02e72c514e3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Conclusion and amplification"}], "description": "Gist: Classical rhetoric offers practical tools for discovering and ordering arguments."}, {"id": "bfeb18be-9625-4438-bdf8-bcf8f3138585", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Style and delivery", "children": [{"id": "1aa80c73-8688-49be-aff5-68d5cc770e1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Diction"}, {"id": "fd23791c-05f0-4fcb-9e35-29c8252cdff6", "type": "concept", "title": "Figures of speech"}, {"id": "5cfcf131-f9d3-4132-b3c0-03e4139bfc3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhythm and emphasis"}, {"id": "cd30c332-9d66-402a-892e-0fa05a9ac608", "type": "concept", "title": "Voice and delivery"}], "description": "Gist: Classical style connects language choices to memory, force, clarity, and audience experience."}]}, {"id": "195af489-ecfb-4626-8ccb-6da3441a6b3c", "type": "topic", "title": "Argument, Claims, Warrants, and the Toulmin Model", "description": "Build sophisticated arguments by connecting claims, evidence, warrants, backing, qualifiers, and rebuttals.", "children": [{"id": "fb10b9cc-b8ed-4f9d-882d-2a9212559892", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Claim design", "children": [{"id": "7048dfbf-32ae-409c-8da1-7e5b2c26f5ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Fact claims"}, {"id": "4c57663b-1c85-4e1f-ae39-cf1904ff9829", "type": "concept", "title": "Value claims"}, {"id": "6a5068a6-adbd-4b1b-906f-b994170fcd34", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy claims"}, {"id": "a7015e6a-5623-4095-96aa-fd3b60eb1080", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis precision"}], "description": "Gist: Strong arguments begin with claims that are specific, contestable, significant, and appropriately scoped."}, {"id": "879d4ca5-a891-4fb7-9de1-eace054d8a4d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Toulmin reasoning", "children": [{"id": "7d5989c7-9fd0-4155-80d9-613014cd27b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Grounds"}, {"id": 